{
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "score": 3,
  "estimated_minutes": 30,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer need only understand that Aspect sought to prevent its former technology executive from working for competitor Avaya under a twelve-month restrictive covenant."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The judged issue concerns ordinary contractual vagueness and ambiguity, not telecommunications technology or the more elaborate Massachusetts–California choice-of-law discussion."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The recorded defect centers on “reasonably likely” in Section 7(a)(iv), and no other provision or separate agreement is needed to understand that challenge."
    },
    "agent_claims": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "j01 combines an objection to the speculative likelihood standard and business scope in clause (iv) with a separate objection to clause (ii)’s hiring prohibition."
    },
    "judge_call": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must decide whether the agent’s speculative-likelihood objection identifies lexical uncertainty or merely challenges breadth, which the court expressly distinguished from Barnett’s argument."
    },
    "volume": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "There is only one judged item, but the lengthy opinion, amendment ruling, full employment agreement, and 52 contextual flags require selective reading."
    }
  },
  "hardest_item": "j01 is the only judged item and requires the most attention because shared reliance on “reasonably likely” does not by itself establish that the agent identified the court’s vagueness issue. The reviewer must separate that possible overlap from the agent’s business-scope and hiring-bar objections.",
  "reason": "This is a bounded, single-item review suitable for a general contract litigator. The decisive comparison is between j01 and opinion lines 43–45, with the later amendment ruling confirming that the same specificity objection resurfaced. The reviewer should distinguish identifying a challenge the court considered from agreeing with that challenge, since the court rejected the asserted vagueness at the preliminary-injunction stage. The surrounding flags warrant a contextual skim, but they do not create a multi-provision defect or require independent review of the equity, compensation, or choice-of-law issues."
}
